// CPACC Preparation Course lesson data.
// Source lesson: Why Disability Models Matter
// This file intentionally contains one lesson only.
window.CPACC_DOMAIN_1_LESSON_01 = {
  "title": "Why Disability Models Matter",  "libraryTopics": ["Disability Models", "Medical Model", "Social Model", "Human Rights Model", "Charity Model", "Biopsychosocial Model"],
  "time": "90 to 120 minutes",
  "parts": [
    {
      "title": "Lesson Overview",
      "body": "Disability models are frameworks people use to explain disability, responsibility, access, and participation. A model is not just an academic label. It quietly shapes what an organization notices, what it ignores, who it believes, where it places responsibility, and what it considers a complete solution.\n\nIn professional accessibility work, models matter because they affect real decisions. A school that sees disability only as an individual medical condition may treat accessible course materials as a special favor for one student. An employer that understands disability as an interaction between a person and an inaccessible environment is more likely to ask why the hiring platform, training documents, meeting process, and workplace tools were not usable from the start. A government agency that understands disability as a civil rights issue is more likely to think about equal access, public participation, procurement, policy, and accountability.\n\nThis lesson introduces the major disability models that will appear throughout Domain 1 of the Certified Professional in Accessibility Core Competencies (CPACC) body of knowledge. The purpose is not to memorize a list of model names. The purpose is to learn how each model changes the question being asked.\n\nA medical model question may ask, \"What condition does this person have?\" A social model question may ask, \"What barrier is preventing participation?\" A human rights model question may ask, \"Is this person being denied dignity, autonomy, equality, or full participation?\" A charity model question may ask, \"How can we help this unfortunate person?\" An economic model question may ask, \"How does disability affect productivity, cost, hiring, benefits, or workforce participation?\" A functional solutions approach may ask, \"What practical support, tool, adaptation, or design change will make the task possible?\" A biopsychosocial approach may ask, \"How do health, individual function, environment, support, policy, and social context interact?\"\n\nThink Like an Accessibility Professional\n\nAn accessibility professional does not stop at identifying the model. The professional asks what the model causes the organization to do next. Does it lead to barrier removal, or only a workaround? Does it respect the disabled person's autonomy, or does it make decisions for them? Does it change a system, or does it treat each disabled person as a new exception? Does it produce access that is private, fragile, delayed, and dependent on special permission, or access that is built into the ordinary experience?\n\nThe most important skill in this lesson is model recognition through consequences. In consulting, the model often hides inside ordinary language. Phrases such as \"we will help them if they ask,\" \"that user needs special treatment,\" \"the software works for most people,\" \"we cannot support every disability,\" or \"accessibility is only required after someone requests an accommodation\" all reveal assumptions. Those assumptions influence design, procurement, training, policy, and service delivery.\n\nBy the end of this lesson, the learner should be able to explain why disability models matter, recognize model-based thinking in professional scenarios, and describe how different models can lead to very different accessibility outcomes. The learner should also begin to see that accessibility work is not simply about disability categories. It is about the relationship between people, environments, technology, policy, communication, and power."
    },
    {
      "title": "Opening Scenario",
      "body": "A blind employee opens a required timekeeping system. Several controls announce only as \"button\" to the screen reader. The employee cannot determine which button saves the timesheet, which button cancels the entry, and which button submits the final record. The visual layout may appear obvious to a sighted employee, but the programmatic information needed by a screen reader user is missing.\n\nThe barrier is not that the employee is blind. Blindness is part of the user's experience, but the work task becomes inaccessible because the system does not expose meaningful control names. The same employee may use email, spreadsheets, calendars, web applications, mobile apps, and professional tools effectively all day. The breakdown occurs when the environment stops communicating necessary information.\n\nWhat the Organization Saw\n\nOne supervisor saw a personal difficulty and assigned a coworker to help every Friday. This may have allowed the timesheet to be submitted, but it made the employee dependent on another person for a private work task. It also did not fix the software, did not protect the next employee, and did not address whether the organization had created an unequal employment process.\n\nAnother supervisor saw a technical defect and asked the software team to add accessible names to the buttons. This response moved closer to accessibility because it addressed the barrier in the product. However, if the organization only fixes that one screen, the same failure may appear again in other systems.\n\nA procurement manager saw a purchasing problem and asked why the product was approved without accessibility requirements. This question shifts attention from one user and one defect to the organization's acquisition process. It asks whether accessibility was considered before money, contracts, implementation, and staff dependence entered the picture.\n\nA compliance officer saw a civil rights and employment issue. If a required workplace system is inaccessible, the employee may not have equal access to the conditions of employment. This question is not only technical. It involves policy, legal risk, dignity, privacy, independence, and equal participation.\n\nWhat the User Experienced\n\nThe employee experienced uncertainty, delay, dependence, and loss of control. The user may have had to reveal the problem repeatedly, ask for assistance with a routine task, trust another person with work time information, or worry that the issue would be interpreted as a performance problem. This is why accessibility analysis must include user impact, not only defect description.\n\nConsultant Thinking\n\nA consultant would separate the immediate support need from the long-term accessibility obligation. The employee may need a reliable way to submit the timesheet today. That does not mean the organization can stop at a workaround. A complete recommendation would likely include fixing the accessible names, testing the full timekeeping workflow with screen reader users, checking related systems for the same pattern, updating procurement requirements, adding accessibility acceptance criteria, and creating a process for employees to report barriers without being treated as the problem.\n\nAccessibility Insight\n\nThe same scenario can be interpreted through several disability models. The medical model may focus on the employee's blindness. The social model focuses on the inaccessible interface. The human rights model focuses on equal employment participation. The functional solutions approach focuses on the practical fix needed to complete the task. The economic model may examine lost productivity and inefficient coworker assistance. The strongest professional response can understand all of those perspectives while refusing to confuse a workaround with full access."
    },
    {
      "title": "Core Learning",
      "body": "A disability model is a way of thinking about disability. It is not a diagnosis, a law, a technical checklist, a medical record, or a personal identity by itself. Models influence how people explain barriers, what they believe disabled people need, and what they believe organizations owe.\n\nAccessibility professionals need to recognize models because models appear inside everyday decisions. A help desk may treat a disabled customer's barrier as an unusual support ticket instead of evidence that a product flow is inaccessible. A school may treat accessible textbooks as an individual accommodation instead of a publishing requirement. A workplace may treat captioning as a courtesy instead of a communication access obligation. A software team may treat keyboard access as an enhancement instead of a baseline interaction requirement.\n\nModels also affect power. Some models give disabled people more control over their own participation. Other models give control to doctors, charities, employers, educators, service providers, technology vendors, or government agencies. When control moves away from disabled people, access can become conditional. The person must ask, explain, prove, wait, disclose, or accept whatever solution someone else chooses.\n\nMajor Model Patterns\n\nThe medical model locates disability primarily in the person's body, mind, or health condition. It often emphasizes diagnosis, treatment, cure, rehabilitation, or professional medical authority. This model can be useful when someone needs health care, therapy, pain management, medication, assistive devices, or clinical support. However, it becomes limiting when an organization uses it to explain away barriers that the organization created.\n\nThe social model locates disability in the interaction between people and barriers in the environment. A wheelchair user is not prevented from entering a building because they use a wheelchair. They are prevented from entering because the building has stairs and no accessible route. A screen reader user is not prevented from submitting a form because they are blind. They are prevented because the form is not coded, labeled, structured, or tested correctly. The social model is especially important in accessibility because it directs attention toward barrier removal.\n\nThe human rights model emphasizes dignity, equality, autonomy, participation, and non-discrimination. It recognizes disabled people as rights holders, not as objects of pity or problems to be managed. This model is important in public services, education, employment, transportation, voting, health care, housing, communication, and digital access because it asks whether disabled people can participate on equal terms.\n\nThe charity model frames disabled people as recipients of kindness, generosity, inspiration, or rescue. It may appear warm on the surface, but it can reduce disabled people to objects of pity and make access dependent on goodwill instead of rights and design. Charity may provide short-term help, but it is not the same as accessibility, equality, or inclusion.\n\nThe economic model examines disability through cost, employment, productivity, benefits, labor participation, and financial impact. This model can reveal important issues, such as the cost of exclusion, inaccessible hiring systems, unemployment, underemployment, and the business value of accessible design. However, it becomes dangerous if it values disabled people only according to productivity or cost avoidance.\n\nThe functional solutions model focuses on what a person needs to do and what solution will make that task possible. This approach is practical and often useful in assistive technology, workplace accommodations, education, rehabilitation, and product design. Its strength is task-level problem solving. Its weakness appears when organizations solve one person's immediate task while leaving the larger barrier in place.\n\nThe biopsychosocial model considers biological, psychological, and social factors together. It can help professionals understand complex experiences where health, environment, support, fatigue, stigma, communication, policy, transportation, technology, and social expectations interact. This model is useful because real life rarely fits into one clean category.\n\nAccessibility Reflection\n\nProfessional accessibility practice often requires more than one model. A person may need medical care and civil rights protection. A student may need an individual accommodation and a redesigned course system. An employee may need assistive technology and an accessible workplace platform. A transit rider may need reliable service information and a transportation policy that does not exclude them.\n\nThe mistake is not using a model. Everyone uses models, whether they name them or not. The mistake is allowing one model to shrink the problem until the organization no longer sees its own responsibility. Accessibility professionals learn to notice when a model helps, when it harms, and when it must be balanced with another perspective."
    },
    {
      "title": "Barrier Thinking",
      "body": "Barrier thinking is the professional habit of asking what prevents participation. This does not deny disability, impairment, health conditions, or individual needs. Instead, it refuses to treat the disabled person as the only location of the problem. It looks at the full environment where the task takes place.\n\nA barrier can appear in a website, mobile application, classroom, workplace, document, meeting, hiring process, transportation system, voting process, emergency service, retail checkout, health portal, public notice, training video, customer support process, or physical building. Barriers can be technical, architectural, communication-based, attitudinal, procedural, financial, sensory, cognitive, organizational, or policy-based.\n\nA person-centered response asks what support the individual needs. This can be important, especially when the person needs an accommodation, assistive technology, personal assistance, medical support, or a specific communication method. A barrier-centered response asks what blocked participation. A rights-centered response asks whether the person had equal opportunity, dignity, privacy, autonomy, and full participation. A program-centered response asks how the organization can prevent the same barrier from returning.\n\nBarrier Analysis Pattern\n\nFirst, identify the task. What is the person trying to do? Examples include applying for a job, reading a notice, attending a meeting, completing training, paying a bill, scheduling a medical appointment, registering for school, voting, evacuating during an emergency, or using public transportation.\n\nSecond, identify the required information, interaction, or environment. Does the person need labels, headings, captions, sign language interpretation, Communication Access Realtime Translation (CART), plain language, tactile signage, keyboard access, accessible transportation, quiet space, flexible scheduling, or compatible assistive technology?\n\nThird, identify what failed. Did the document lack structure? Did the video lack captions? Did the meeting platform fail with a screen reader? Did the form time out? Did the building lack an accessible route? Did the policy require a phone call when the user communicates more effectively in writing? Did staff treat an accommodation request as an inconvenience?\n\nFourth, identify the impact. Was the person delayed, excluded, embarrassed, made dependent, forced to disclose private information, denied service, prevented from working, unable to learn, unable to communicate, or placed at risk?\n\nFifth, recommend both immediate and systemic action. Immediate support may be necessary, but systemic prevention is the professional target. A one-time manual fix is a bandage. A changed workflow is a hinge repair. The door should not squeak every time a disabled person arrives.\n\nWhat the Organization Saw\n\nOrganizations often see one complaint, one ticket, one student, one employee, one customer, or one deadline. They may ask, \"How do we help this person right now?\" That question matters, but it is incomplete.\n\nWhat the User Experienced\n\nThe user often experiences a pattern. The inaccessible document is not just one document. It may be the latest example of inaccessible notices, forms, portals, meetings, and service channels. The user may have already spent years navigating systems that require extra labor simply to receive ordinary access.\n\nConsultant Thinking\n\nA consultant listens for the difference between a symptom and a system. If one Portable Document Format (PDF) file is inaccessible, the symptom is that one file. The system may be an authoring process, template problem, procurement gap, staff training issue, missing quality assurance step, or lack of leadership accountability. If one form field is unlabeled, the symptom is a failed field. The system may be a development pattern, component library defect, design system omission, or testing process that excludes assistive technology.\n\nThink Like an Accessibility Professional\n\nThe professional question is not only \"What disability does the user have?\" The stronger question is \"What conditions must exist for this person to participate with dignity, independence, reliability, and equal opportunity?\" That question moves accessibility from reaction to design, from pity to rights, and from exception handling to organizational maturity."
    },
    {
      "title": "Real World Example",
      "body": "Real-world accessibility work rarely announces which disability model is operating. The model is usually hidden inside decisions, timelines, budgets, job descriptions, procurement language, training materials, customer service scripts, and the way an organization explains a barrier after it has already harmed someone.\n\nThe following examples show how disability models shape action. Each example should be read as a professional reasoning exercise, not as a story about one disability category. The same pattern can appear across physical disabilities, sensory disabilities, cognitive disabilities, learning disabilities, mental health disabilities, chronic health conditions, speech disabilities, Deaf (capital D) cultural identity, deaf (lowercase d) hearing status, DeafBlindness, neurodivergence, and temporary or situational limitations.\n\nCase Study 1: Emergency Shelter Information\n\nA city posts emergency shelter information as an image-only Portable Document Format (PDF). The document contains shelter names, addresses, eligibility notes, transportation details, and phone numbers. A sighted person can visually read the image. A screen reader user hears little or nothing useful because the text is not available programmatically. A person using text enlargement may struggle because the image becomes blurry when magnified. A person with a cognitive disability may struggle because the document uses dense emergency language without plain-language structure. A person with limited bandwidth may not be able to load the large PDF quickly on a mobile device.\n\nWhat the Organization Saw\n\nThe emergency management office saw a document that looked finished. Staff may have believed the information was public because it was posted online. They may have assumed that a phone number was enough for anyone who could not read the document. They may also have believed that accessibility could wait because the emergency was urgent.\n\nWhat the User Experienced\n\nThe user experienced blocked access at the exact moment when timing mattered. The issue was not only inconvenience. The person may have been unable to determine where to go, whether transportation was available, whether a service animal was allowed, whether medical equipment could be charged, or whether the shelter could meet communication needs. The barrier could affect safety, health, family coordination, and trust in public services.\n\nConsultant Thinking\n\nA weak response would be to say, \"Call us if you need help.\" That may provide a temporary channel, but it does not create equal access. It also assumes the user can make a phone call, wait on hold, communicate by voice, and receive accurate information from staff under pressure.\n\nA stronger response separates immediate remediation from prevention. The city should provide the emergency information in HyperText Markup Language (HTML) on a web page, because HTML is usually the most flexible format for screen readers, magnification, reflow, mobile access, translation, and search. If a PDF is also provided, it should be properly tagged, have a logical reading order, include real text rather than an image of text, and be tested with assistive technology. The city should also establish an emergency publishing policy that requires accessible source templates, plain-language review, captioned videos, accessible maps or text alternatives for map information, and a pre-publication accessibility check before emergency content goes live.\n\nAccessibility Insight\n\nThe medical model might focus on the individual user's blindness. The social model identifies the inaccessible document and publishing process as the barrier. The human rights model asks whether disabled residents have equal access to emergency information. The functional solutions approach asks what format will allow the task to be completed immediately. The organizational accessibility perspective asks how the city can prevent inaccessible emergency communication from recurring.\n\nCase Study 2: Hiring Platform With Timed Assessments\n\nAn employer uses an online hiring platform that requires applicants to complete a timed assessment. The assessment has unlabeled form fields, keyboard traps, drag-and-drop activities, auto-advancing questions, and instructions that are conveyed only through color. The employer states that applicants who need accommodations can contact Human Resources.\n\nWhat the Organization Saw\n\nThe employer saw a standardized hiring process. The platform vendor described the assessment as efficient, objective, and scalable. Human Resources believed that the accommodation statement solved the accessibility issue because applicants could ask for help.\n\nWhat the User Experienced\n\nA keyboard-only applicant may become trapped in a component and lose time. A blind applicant may not know what an unlabeled field requires. A color-blind applicant may miss instructions that depend on red and green. A person with a learning disability, traumatic brain injury, or anxiety disability may be disadvantaged by rigid timing and auto-advancing content. A person who uses speech recognition may not be able to complete drag-and-drop activities. The applicant may be forced to disclose disability before being considered for the job, which can create fear, stigma, and unequal treatment.\n\nConsultant Thinking\n\nThe professional issue is not only whether accommodations are available. The deeper issue is whether the hiring process itself creates unnecessary barriers. A consultant would ask whether the assessment is job-related, whether the same skill can be measured in an accessible way, whether the vendor provided evidence of accessibility conformance, whether keyboard and screen reader testing were performed, whether timing is essential, and whether applicants can request accommodation without penalty, delay, or disclosure to decision makers who do not need that information.\n\nA strong recommendation may include procurement review, vendor accessibility documentation, user testing with assistive technology, alternatives for inaccessible interactions, review of timed requirements, staff training, and a policy that accessibility must be evaluated before a platform is used in hiring.\n\nThink Like an Accessibility Professional\n\nAn accommodation process is not a substitute for accessible design. Accommodations are important, but they should not become the organization's excuse for purchasing inaccessible systems. When a barrier is predictable, repeated, and built into the ordinary process, professional accessibility practice pushes upstream.\n\nCase Study 3: College Course Videos Without Captions\n\nA college instructor uploads lecture videos without captions or transcripts. A deaf (lowercase d) student who relies on captions cannot access the lectures. A Deaf (capital D) student whose primary language is American Sign Language may need captions and, in some contexts, sign language interpretation. A student with attention-related disabilities may use captions to reinforce comprehension. A student in a noisy home may use captions because audio is difficult to hear. A student whose first language is not English may use captions to support vocabulary recognition.\n\nWhat the Organization Saw\n\nThe instructor saw a video library. The disability services office saw an accommodation request. The learning technology team saw a media workflow. The legal office may have seen compliance risk. Each perspective contains part of the truth, but no single perspective is enough.\n\nWhat the User Experienced\n\nThe student experienced delayed access to course content. If captions are added only after an accommodation request, the student may fall behind while waiting. The student may also have to identify themselves repeatedly, coordinate with multiple offices, and carry the burden of fixing a course design problem.\n\nConsultant Thinking\n\nA consultant would distinguish between individual accommodation and institutional accessibility. A student may need a specific accommodation, such as Communication Access Realtime Translation (CART), sign language interpretation, extended time, or an alternative assignment format. At the same time, the institution should have a media policy requiring captions for instructional videos, accurate transcripts when needed, accessible media players, quality standards for captions, and a workflow that prevents inaccessible videos from becoming part of a course.\n\nAccessibility Insight\n\nCaptions are not only a disability accommodation. They are part of accessible communication infrastructure. They benefit deaf and hard of hearing people, some Deaf signers, people with auditory processing disabilities, people in noisy environments, people in quiet environments where audio cannot be played, language learners, and anyone who needs searchable text. This is a classic example of how barrier removal can support many users without reducing disabled people to a special case.\n\nCase Study 4: Workplace Return-to-Office Policy\n\nAn organization announces a mandatory return-to-office policy. The policy requires all employees to work in the office five days a week, attend meetings in person, use a specific parking garage, and reserve workstations through a mobile app. The organization states that exceptions will be considered only with medical documentation.\n\nWhat the Organization Saw\n\nLeadership saw culture, productivity, supervision, and space utilization. Human Resources saw policy administration. Facilities saw seating and parking. Information Technology saw the reservation app. The organization may not have recognized that the policy combines physical access, transportation, fatigue, chronic health conditions, sensory environment, communication access, caregiving logistics, mobility, and technology.\n\nWhat the User Experienced\n\nA wheelchair user may encounter an inaccessible route from the garage. A person with chronic pain or fatigue may be harmed by commuting five days a week. A neurodivergent employee may experience sensory overload in an open office. A blind employee may find the desk reservation app inaccessible. A hard of hearing employee may lose access when hybrid meetings become poor-quality in-room conversations. A person with an immune-related disability may face health risks in crowded spaces.\n\nConsultant Thinking\n\nA consultant would not reduce the issue to one employee needing an exception. The policy should be analyzed as a system. Does the organization provide accessible routes, accessible parking, accessible digital tools, flexible work options, accessible meeting practices, assistive technology compatibility, quiet workspaces, effective communication, and an accommodation process that is timely and respectful? Are managers trained to understand disability without demanding unnecessary private medical details? Are remote and hybrid options considered accessibility tools rather than rewards?\n\nProfessional Lesson\n\nThe barrier may be created by the interaction of policy, place, technology, and culture. This is why the biopsychosocial model can be useful. Health, environment, organizational expectations, transportation, digital access, and social attitudes all shape participation.\n\nAccessibility Reflection\n\nAcross these examples, the professional pattern is the same. Do not stop at the visible symptom. Ask what the user was trying to do, what blocked the task, who controlled the environment, what model shaped the response, what immediate support is needed, and what systemic change will prevent the barrier from returning."
    },
    {
      "title": "Professional Perspective",
      "body": "Accessibility professionals should be careful not to blame disabled people for barriers created by products, policies, buildings, documents, services, or attitudes. This is easy to say and harder to practice. Many organizations have learned to speak politely about disability while still designing systems that require disabled people to request permission to participate.\n\nProfessional accessibility work requires a shift from personal deficit thinking to barrier analysis. The question is not whether a person can work harder, ask for help, bring their own workaround, use a different device, disclose more information, or wait longer. The stronger question is whether the environment supports equal participation from the beginning.\n\nThe Consultant's Role\n\nA consultant often enters after a barrier has already appeared. A customer could not complete checkout. An employee could not use training. A student could not access course materials. A patient could not use a health portal. A resident could not read a public notice. A job applicant could not complete an assessment. By the time the consultant is involved, the organization may already be defensive.\n\nThe consultant's job is not to shame the organization. The consultant's job is to make the barrier understandable, connect it to user impact, identify root causes, and recommend action that is practical, ethical, and sustainable. That requires language that is clear enough for executives, specific enough for technical teams, respectful enough for disabled users, and grounded enough for legal, procurement, training, and operations teams.\n\nProfessional Language\n\nModel-based thinking shows up in language. \"Confined to a wheelchair\" suggests tragedy and limitation. \"Wheelchair user\" is usually more neutral and accurate. A wheelchair may be the tool that makes mobility possible. \"Suffers from blindness\" centers suffering and may imply that blindness itself explains the access problem. \"Blind screen reader user\" may be relevant when describing a digital barrier, but the barrier should still be located accurately. \"The user could not submit the form because the required fields were not programmatically identified\" is stronger than \"The blind user could not use the form.\"\n\nThis does not mean disability should be hidden. Professional writing should name disability when it is relevant to understanding access needs, testing conditions, or user impact. The point is to avoid language that turns disabled people into problems, inspirations, burdens, or objects of pity.\n\nWorking With Multiple Models\n\nProfessional practice does not require rejecting every model except the social model. The medical model may matter when a person needs treatment, rehabilitation, medication, clinical documentation, or health-related support. The functional solutions approach may matter when selecting assistive technology or adapting a task. The economic model may help explain the cost of inaccessible procurement or employee turnover. The human rights model may be essential when discussing equal access, discrimination, autonomy, dignity, and public participation. The biopsychosocial model may be needed when health, environment, technology, social expectations, and policy interact.\n\nThe professional skill is knowing which model is doing useful work and which model is hiding responsibility. If an organization uses the medical model to understand a person's fatigue and provide flexibility, it may help. If the organization uses the medical model to insist that the person's body is the only problem while refusing to change an unnecessary five-day office policy, it harms. If an organization uses the economic model to justify accessible hiring because it expands talent, that can help. If it uses the economic model to value disabled people only by productivity, that harms.\n\nConsulting Pattern: From Incident to System\n\nWhen an accessibility issue appears, begin with the incident but do not end there. A mature professional response usually includes five layers.\n\nThe first layer is user impact. Describe what the person was prevented from doing and why it matters. Impact should include delay, exclusion, dependence, privacy loss, communication failure, safety risk, lost opportunity, or unequal treatment when relevant.\n\nThe second layer is barrier identification. Describe the specific condition that created the barrier. Examples include missing accessible names, lack of captions, inaccessible PDF structure, no keyboard operation, poor focus order, unlabeled form fields, inaccessible route, policy requiring phone-only communication, or staff refusing an accommodation request.\n\nThe third layer is immediate access. Determine what must happen now so the person can complete the task. This could include an accessible alternative format, captioned media, direct assistance chosen by the user, temporary adjustment, alternate communication channel, or urgent repair.\n\nThe fourth layer is root cause. Ask how the barrier entered the system. Was it a template, vendor product, design pattern, procurement gap, training issue, quality assurance failure, unclear ownership, schedule pressure, or leadership decision?\n\nThe fifth layer is prevention. Recommend the durable change. Prevention may require accessible design standards, procurement requirements, staff training, testing with assistive technology, content governance, policy revision, monitoring, ownership, and accountability.\n\nWhat the Organization Saw\n\nMany organizations see accessibility as a series of individual requests. They may believe they are being responsive because they handle each request when it arrives.\n\nWhat the User Experienced\n\nDisabled users often experience repeated negotiation. They may need to ask for the same access repeatedly in different departments, platforms, forms, meetings, and documents. This creates disability-related labor that the organization may not see.\n\nAccessibility Insight\n\nA professional accessibility recommendation should reduce repeated negotiation wherever possible. Built-in access is more reliable than permission-based access. A captioned video library is stronger than captioning only after a student complains. An accessible hiring platform is stronger than asking applicants to request an exception. An accessible public notice system is stronger than a phone number for people who cannot read the posted file.\n\nThink Like an Accessibility Professional\n\nWhen reviewing a barrier, ask three quiet questions. Who has to do extra work because of this design? Who has to disclose private information to receive ordinary access? Who benefits if the system is fixed instead of patched? Those questions turn model theory into professional judgment."
    },
    {
      "title": "Accessibility Implications",
      "body": "Disability models matter because they influence practical decisions long after a lesson ends. In real accessibility work, professionals rarely hear someone say, \"We are applying the medical model today,\" or \"This policy reflects charity model thinking.\" Instead, the model is hidden inside how an organization designs services, assigns responsibility, sets budgets, trains staff, buys technology, handles complaints, and decides whether a barrier is urgent.\n\nAccessibility implications are the real-world consequences of those assumptions. A model can determine whether an organization treats access as a favor, a compliance task, a design requirement, a civil right, a procurement condition, a customer service issue, or a routine part of quality. The same inaccessible experience can produce very different organizational responses depending on which model is shaping the decision.\n\nEducation\n\nIn education, disability models influence whether access is built into the course or handled only after a student requests accommodation. A school influenced mainly by individual accommodation thinking may wait until a disabled student enrolls before converting materials, captioning videos, providing accessible documents, or checking the learning platform. This approach may technically respond to one student, but it often creates delay, disclosure, and unequal access.\n\nWhat the Organization Saw\n\nThe school may see an accommodation request. It may believe the issue begins when the student asks for help.\n\nWhat the User Experienced\n\nThe student may experience late access, repeated explanation, dependence on disability services, and the pressure of falling behind while everyone else begins the course on time.\n\nConsultant Thinking\n\nA consultant would ask whether accessibility is part of course design, procurement, faculty training, media production, document templates, and learning management system review. The individual accommodation may still matter, but the larger question is whether the educational environment is prepared for disabled learners before they have to ask.\n\nEmployment\n\nIn employment, disability models influence hiring, onboarding, training, performance management, remote work, workplace technology, and advancement. If an employer sees disability only through a medical model, it may focus heavily on documentation and exceptions. If it uses a social and human rights lens, it will also ask whether the hiring platform, interview process, job descriptions, workplace software, meetings, and policies create unnecessary barriers.\n\nAccessibility Insight\n\nAn inaccessible hiring process is not only a technical problem. It can affect who gets considered for employment. It can force applicants to disclose disability early. It can screen out qualified people before the organization ever evaluates their skills. This is why accessibility professionals connect disability models to opportunity, not just usability.\n\nHealthcare\n\nIn healthcare, disability models can determine whether access is treated as part of patient safety and dignity or as an optional service. An inaccessible patient portal, an uncaptioned telehealth visit, a form that cannot be completed with assistive technology, a lack of effective communication, or a policy that requires phone-only scheduling can all affect care.\n\nWhat the User Experienced\n\nA disabled patient may be unable to schedule an appointment, review test results, understand medication instructions, communicate symptoms, or privately manage health information. The barrier may be digital, communication-based, procedural, or attitudinal. The impact may be medical, emotional, financial, and practical at the same time.\n\nConsultant Thinking\n\nThe professional recommendation should not stop at \"provide help on request.\" Healthcare access often requires accessible portals, effective communication policies, staff training, alternative communication channels, accessible documents, captioned or interpreted telehealth, and procurement requirements for medical technology.\n\nGovernment Services and Civic Participation\n\nGovernment accessibility implicates public rights. Residents may need to apply for benefits, attend public meetings, read emergency notices, vote, pay taxes, request services, access transportation information, or participate in civic life. If disability is framed as an individual inconvenience, the government may offer a workaround. If it is framed as equal public participation, the government must examine whether its ordinary systems are accessible.\n\nThink Like an Accessibility Professional\n\nA public agency should not require disabled residents to rely on private assistance to complete public tasks. The professional question is whether the resident can participate with the same dignity, privacy, timeliness, and reliability as others.\n\nProcurement and Technology\n\nProcurement is one of the places where disability models become organizational reality. If accessibility is considered only after purchase, the organization may inherit barriers that are expensive and slow to fix. If accessibility is treated as a core quality requirement, it becomes part of vendor evaluation, contract language, acceptance testing, maintenance, and renewal decisions.\n\nA consultant reviewing procurement should ask whether accessibility requirements were included before purchase, whether the vendor provided credible accessibility documentation, whether assistive technology testing was performed, whether known defects were accepted, and whether the contract includes remediation expectations.\n\nTransportation, Emergency Planning, and Everyday Life\n\nDisability models also shape transportation, emergency planning, housing, retail, banking, entertainment, media, and everyday community participation. A transit agency may see missed paratransit trips as isolated service problems, while users experience lost work, missed medical care, and reduced independence. A streaming service may see captions and audio description as media features, while users experience access to culture, education, family life, and public conversation. A store may see an inaccessible checkout kiosk as a device issue, while users experience dependence, privacy loss, and exclusion from ordinary shopping.\n\nAccessibility Reflection\n\nThe professional implication is clear: disability models are not only ideas. They are engines that drive decisions. They influence who must adapt, who has power, who waits, who discloses, who receives independent access, and who is treated as an exception.\n\nWhen evaluating any service, ask: What model appears to be shaping this system? Does the response place all responsibility on the disabled person? Does it remove the barrier? Does it protect dignity and autonomy? Does it improve the system for future users? These questions turn disability model knowledge into accessibility practice."
    },
    {
      "title": "Common Misconceptions",
      "body": "Because disability models are often taught as named concepts, learners may believe the difficult part is memorizing the definitions. In professional practice, the harder work is recognizing common misunderstandings before they shape poor recommendations. Misconceptions can sound reasonable, especially when they are expressed politely or wrapped in good intentions. Accessibility professionals need to identify them without losing the consulting relationship.\n\nMisconception: The social model denies impairment.\n\nThe social model does not deny that impairments, health conditions, pain, fatigue, sensory differences, mobility differences, cognitive disabilities, mental health disabilities, or communication disabilities are real. It simply refuses to treat the person's body or mind as the only explanation for exclusion.\n\nA wheelchair user's mobility is real. The stairs are also real. A blind user's lack of vision is real. The unlabeled button is also real. A deaf (lowercase d) user's hearing status is real. The uncaptioned video is also real. The social model helps professionals locate the barrier that can be changed.\n\nConsultant Thinking\n\nWhen someone says, \"But the disability is real,\" the consultant does not need to argue. A strong response is: \"Yes, and our responsibility is to identify which parts of the environment, process, technology, or communication are creating preventable exclusion.\"\n\nMisconception: The medical model is always bad.\n\nThe medical model can be harmful when it is used to blame the person or avoid barrier removal. However, medical care, therapy, rehabilitation, medication, clinical documentation, pain management, mobility support, mental health treatment, and health-related expertise may be important parts of someone's life.\n\nThe issue is not whether medicine has value. The issue is whether medical thinking is being used in the wrong place. A doctor may help a person manage a health condition. A web team still needs to label the form field. A clinician may document functional limitations. An employer still needs an accessible training platform. A therapist may support communication development. A school still needs accessible course content.\n\nAccessibility Insight\n\nProfessional accessibility work does not require disrespecting medical experience. It requires making sure medical explanations do not erase organizational responsibility.\n\nMisconception: Accommodations and accessibility are the same.\n\nAccommodations and accessibility are related, but they are not the same. Accessibility is the design of environments, technologies, services, documents, media, and processes so people can use them without unnecessary barriers. Accommodations are individualized adjustments that address a person's specific needs.\n\nA captioned video library is accessibility. Captioning one video after a student requests it may be an accommodation. An accessible hiring platform is accessibility. Giving one applicant a separate process may be an accommodation. A properly structured Portable Document Format (PDF) template is accessibility. Manually fixing one document after a complaint may be accommodation or remediation.\n\nStrong organizations need both. However, accommodations should not be used as an excuse to leave predictable barriers in place.\n\nMisconception: Accessibility is only about compliance.\n\nCompliance matters. Laws, standards, regulations, contracts, and policies can create accountability. However, accessibility is larger than compliance. A technically compliant experience can still be confusing, exhausting, delayed, or unequal. A minimal legal response may reduce risk without creating meaningful inclusion.\n\nThink Like an Accessibility Professional\n\nCompliance asks, \"What are we required to do?\" Accessibility also asks, \"Can people actually participate? Is the experience reliable? Is it private? Is it respectful? Does it work across real users, real devices, real assistive technologies, and real contexts?\"\n\nMisconception: Accessibility is mostly about blindness.\n\nBlindness and screen reader access are important, but accessibility is much broader. Disability can involve mobility, dexterity, hearing, vision, speech, cognition, learning, memory, attention, mental health, chronic illness, pain, fatigue, seizure disorders, neurodivergence, Deaf (capital D) cultural identity, deaf (lowercase d) hearing status, DeafBlindness, and multiple disabilities at once.\n\nA professional who treats accessibility as only screen reader testing will miss captions, color contrast, plain language, keyboard access, touch target size, cognitive load, motion sensitivity, speech input, sensory environment, accessible routes, and effective communication.\n\nMisconception: One solution works for everyone in a disability category.\n\nDisability categories can help identify possible access needs, but they do not define every user. Two blind users may use different screen readers, braille displays, magnification, mobile devices, keyboard strategies, or workflows. Two people with the same diagnosis may experience different barriers. Two Deaf people may have different language preferences, communication preferences, and cultural identities.\n\nProfessional recommendations should avoid assuming that one person's solution represents an entire community. Instead, consultants should identify the task, the barrier, the user's access needs, and the system change that will support a wider range of users.\n\nMisconception: Help is the same as equality.\n\nHelp may be necessary and welcome in some moments. But help is not automatically equal access. If a person must ask staff to read private information, complete a form, operate a kiosk, navigate a website, or submit a workplace record, the person may lose privacy, independence, reliability, and dignity.\n\nThe professional question is not whether staff are kind. The question is whether the system requires disabled people to depend on kindness when others receive direct access.\n\nAccessibility Reflection\n\nMisconceptions matter because they become design decisions. They appear in policies, budgets, job descriptions, vendor contracts, course development, customer service scripts, and remediation priorities. A consultant who can identify misconceptions early can redirect the conversation before an organization builds another barrier into the system.\n\nBefore moving into exam preparation, pause on this lesson's central idea: disability models are not labels for people. They are tools for analyzing responsibility. They help professionals ask better questions, make better recommendations, and support access that is ordinary rather than exceptional."
    },
    {
      "title": "CPACC Exam Focus",
      "body": "For the Certified Professional in Accessibility Core Competencies (CPACC) exam, disability models are likely to appear as scenario-based reasoning rather than isolated vocabulary. The exam may not simply ask, \"What is the social model?\" It may describe a workplace, classroom, service counter, health portal, transportation system, or government process and expect the learner to recognize where responsibility is being placed.\n\nThe most useful way to prepare is to study the models as patterns of responsibility. Ask what the scenario treats as the problem. Ask who is expected to change. Ask whether the proposed solution removes a barrier, provides a temporary workaround, protects dignity, or leaves the disabled person dependent on special permission.\n\nMedical Model Exam Pattern\n\nThe medical model focuses on the person's impairment, diagnosis, treatment, cure, therapy, rehabilitation, or medical documentation. Exam answers that place the main explanation inside the person's body or mind may reflect this model.\n\nA medical model answer might say that a person cannot participate because they are blind, deaf, have limited mobility, have a learning disability, have chronic pain, or have a mental health disability. A more accessibility-centered answer will usually look for the barrier: the unlabeled form field, missing captions, inaccessible route, complex instructions, inflexible attendance policy, unsupported assistive technology, or staff process that requires communication in only one way.\n\nImportant exam nuance: the medical model is not always wrong. Medical care, rehabilitation, therapy, medication, and clinical support may be essential in some contexts. The issue is whether the medical model is being used in a situation where barrier removal, equal access, autonomy, or civil rights should be central. For example, medical documentation may support an accommodation request, but documentation alone does not make the workplace software accessible.\n\nSocial Model Exam Pattern\n\nThe social model focuses on barriers created by the environment, technology, communication, policy, design, or attitudes. Exam answers that identify the inaccessible condition and recommend removing the barrier often reflect this model.\n\nLook for language about accessible routes, captions, screen reader compatibility, keyboard operation, plain language, accessible documents, inclusive communication, flexible processes, accessible transportation, and barrier removal. The social model shifts the question from \"What is wrong with the person?\" to \"What is blocking participation?\"\n\nA strong exam answer may say that a wheelchair user is excluded by stairs without an accessible route, not by the wheelchair. It may say that a screen reader user is blocked by missing labels and poor semantic structure, not by blindness alone. It may say that a deaf (lowercase d) user is excluded by an uncaptioned video, not by hearing status alone.\n\nHuman Rights Model Exam Pattern\n\nThe human rights model focuses on dignity, equality, autonomy, non-discrimination, and full participation. Exam answers that emphasize equal opportunity, independent participation, privacy, choice, and freedom from discrimination may reflect this model.\n\nThis model is especially important in scenarios involving education, employment, government services, voting, transportation, health care, housing, emergency communication, public meetings, and justice systems. If the scenario asks why accessibility matters beyond convenience, the human rights model may be the strongest frame.\n\nFor example, an inaccessible voting machine is not simply a technical inconvenience. It may affect privacy, independence, and equal civic participation. An inaccessible patient portal is not simply a usability issue. It may affect health information, appointment scheduling, medication instructions, privacy, and timely care.\n\nCharity Model Exam Pattern\n\nThe charity model frames disabled people as people to be helped, pitied, rescued, admired for inspiration, or supported through generosity. Exam scenarios may include well-meaning language that still removes power from disabled people.\n\nA charity model response may provide help without changing the barrier. For example, a restaurant employee reads an inaccessible menu aloud every time a blind customer visits, but the restaurant never provides an accessible menu. The assistance may be kind in the moment, but it does not create independent, reliable, private access.\n\nOn the exam, be careful with answers that sound warm but keep the disabled person dependent. CPACC questions often reward respect for autonomy over sympathy.\n\nEconomic Model Exam Pattern\n\nThe economic model focuses on cost, productivity, employment, benefits, workforce participation, efficiency, and financial impact. It may appear in workplace, hiring, procurement, or business scenarios.\n\nThis model can be useful when explaining the cost of inaccessible procurement, employee turnover, lost customers, reduced productivity, litigation risk, or exclusion from the labor market. It can also support the business case for accessibility. However, disabled people should not be valued only according to economic output.\n\nA balanced answer may recognize that accessibility improves business performance while still grounding the obligation in dignity, equal participation, and rights.\n\nFunctional Solutions Exam Pattern\n\nA functional solutions approach focuses on what the person needs to do and what support, tool, adaptation, or design change will make the task possible. This pattern often appears in assistive technology and accommodation scenarios.\n\nFor example, if an employee cannot use a standard mouse, a functional response may consider keyboard access, switch access, speech recognition, alternative pointing devices, or changes to the software. If a student cannot access printed course material, a functional response may consider accessible electronic text, braille, audio, tactile graphics, or structured documents.\n\nThe exam may reward practical matching of solution to task. However, remember that a one-person solution does not always fix the underlying system. A functional workaround may be necessary today, while a broader accessibility fix is still required for tomorrow.\n\nBiopsychosocial Exam Pattern\n\nThe biopsychosocial model considers biological, psychological, and social factors together. It may appear in scenarios involving chronic illness, mental health, pain, fatigue, neurodivergence, environmental stress, stigma, support systems, policy, transportation, and participation.\n\nThis model is useful when a scenario cannot be explained by one factor alone. For example, an employee's participation may be affected by a health condition, office lighting, commute demands, meeting culture, inaccessible software, manager expectations, and fatigue. The model helps professionals avoid oversimplifying.\n\nCommon Exam Traps\n\nOne common trap is choosing the answer that sounds helpful but keeps the disabled person dependent. If the answer provides personal assistance without fixing the barrier, examine whether it reflects charity rather than accessibility.\n\nAnother trap is choosing the answer that focuses only on diagnosis when the scenario is really about an inaccessible environment. If a student cannot access an online quiz because it times out too quickly and cannot be operated by keyboard, the stronger answer is not about the student's disability label. It is about the design barrier.\n\nA third trap is treating accommodation as the whole solution. Accommodations matter, but accessible design and inclusive policy reduce the need for repeated individual requests.\n\nA fourth trap is assuming one disability equals one solution. Captions support deaf and hard of hearing users, but they can also support users with auditory processing disabilities, language learners, people in noisy environments, and people who need searchable text. Screen reader compatibility supports blind users, but semantic structure also helps navigation, automation, mobile adaptation, and quality control.\n\nCPACC Study Strategy\n\nWhen reading a scenario, identify the task, the barrier, the user impact, and the responsibility. Then ask which answer moves from personal blame toward access, participation, dignity, and barrier removal.\n\nIf two answers seem possible, prefer the answer that is more systemic, more respectful of autonomy, and more focused on equal participation. The strongest answer usually recognizes disability as part of human diversity and accessibility as a professional responsibility.\n\nThink Like an Accessibility Professional\n\nDo not memorize the models as a row of flashcards. Practice hearing them inside real decisions. \"They can ask for help\" may reveal charity or accommodation-only thinking. \"We need medical proof before we consider changing the policy\" may reveal medical model dominance. \"The form blocks keyboard users, so the form must be repaired\" reflects social model barrier analysis. \"Residents must receive emergency information in a form they can use privately and quickly\" reflects human rights thinking.\n\nThat is the exam skill and the professional skill: recognize the assumption, locate the barrier, and choose the response that supports equal participation."
    },
    {
      "title": "Lesson Summary",
      "body": "Disability models shape how people understand disability, responsibility, access, and participation. They influence what an organization notices, what it ignores, who it believes, and what it considers a complete solution. For accessibility professionals, models are not abstract theory. They are practical tools for analyzing barriers, explaining user impact, and improving decisions.\n\nThe medical model focuses on impairment, diagnosis, treatment, cure, rehabilitation, and medical authority. It can be useful when health care or clinical support is relevant. It becomes harmful when it is used to explain away barriers created by environments, technology, communication, policy, or attitudes.\n\nThe social model focuses on barriers that prevent participation. It is central to accessibility because it directs attention toward inaccessible buildings, documents, websites, applications, media, transportation systems, services, attitudes, and organizational processes. It helps professionals ask what must change in the environment so that disabled people are not forced to carry the burden alone.\n\nThe human rights model focuses on dignity, equality, autonomy, non-discrimination, and full participation. It reminds professionals that accessibility is not a favor, a courtesy, or a bonus feature. It is connected to civil rights, equal opportunity, privacy, independence, communication, safety, and public life.\n\nThe charity model may provide help, but it often frames disabled people as objects of pity or generosity. It can leave access dependent on goodwill instead of rights, design, and accountability. Professional accessibility work should not confuse kindness with equality. A person may appreciate help in the moment and still deserve a system that does not require help every time.\n\nThe economic model examines cost, productivity, workforce participation, benefits, and financial impact. It can support strong business arguments for accessibility, especially in hiring, procurement, customer experience, and employee retention. Its limitation is that it can reduce disabled people's value to economic output if it is not balanced with human rights and dignity.\n\nThe functional solutions model focuses on practical task completion. It is useful for accommodations, assistive technology, education, employment, rehabilitation, and immediate problem solving. Its limitation is that it may solve one person's task while leaving the system inaccessible for others.\n\nThe biopsychosocial model considers biological, psychological, and social factors together. It is useful because disability experience often involves the interaction of health, environment, support, stigma, technology, policy, transportation, communication, and social expectations.\n\nThe professional lesson is that no model should be used blindly. Accessibility professionals need to recognize which model is shaping a decision and whether that model leads toward or away from access. The strongest professional analysis usually combines practical support for the individual with systemic change that prevents the barrier from recurring.\n\nA learner completing this lesson should now be able to do more than name the models. The learner should be able to hear model-based assumptions in ordinary language, recognize whether responsibility is being placed only on the disabled person, explain why barrier removal matters, and recommend solutions that support dignity, independence, reliability, privacy, and equal participation.\n\nProfessional Integration\n\nThis lesson also establishes a consulting pattern that will return throughout the course. Begin by identifying the task. Identify the barrier. Describe the user impact. Ask who controls the environment. Determine whether the current response is a workaround, an accommodation, a design correction, a policy change, or a rights-based solution. Then recommend action that addresses both immediate access and long-term prevention.\n\nFor example, an inaccessible Portable Document Format (PDF) may require an accessible version today. But the long-term recommendation should also examine templates, author training, document governance, approval workflows, and quality assurance. An unlabeled button may require a code fix today. But the long-term recommendation should also examine the design system, component library, developer training, testing process, and procurement expectations. A missing caption file may require urgent captioning today. But the long-term recommendation should also examine media policy, vendor selection, caption quality standards, and course development workflows.\n\nAccessibility Reflection\n\nWhen a disabled person encounters a barrier, the professional question is not simply \"What disability does this person have?\" The stronger question is \"What conditions must exist for this person to participate fully, safely, privately, independently, and with equal opportunity?\" That question is the bridge from disability model theory to accessibility practice.\n\nThis lesson prepares the learner for the rest of Domain 1. Future lessons will examine disability experience, identity, language, assistive technology, culture, intersectionality, demographics, and the social meaning of disability. The models introduced here will continue to matter because they shape every later topic. They are the mental map beneath the work.\n\nThink Like an Accessibility Professional\n\nA professional does not use disability models to label people. A professional uses them to analyze decisions. The goal is not to decide whether a person fits a model. The goal is to recognize when an organization is using a model that hides responsibility, delays access, reduces autonomy, or treats disabled people as exceptions.\n\nThe strongest accessibility work moves in the opposite direction. It makes access ordinary. It reduces the need for repeated disclosure. It protects independence. It respects lived experience. It improves systems. It helps organizations understand that accessibility is not an afterthought at the edge of quality. Accessibility is one of the ways quality proves itself."
    }
  ]
};
